text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"base’s row "},{"code":"i","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" lacks, ascending: the row iterator marks the base row in a mask of ⌈n\/64⌉ words,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"O(deg + n\/64), then yields the clear bits. A row costs O(n\/64 + output) and one allocation."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"The complement of a sparse graph has Θ(n²) edges, so a traversal is Θ(n²) whatever the layout."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"An edge’s position is its cell of the adjacency matrix, "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Cell(source: i, target: j)"},{"type":"text","text":", ordered"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"row-major as "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"AdjacencyMatrix"},{"type":"text","text":"’s positions are; an undirected edge has "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"source < target"},{"type":"text","text":". There"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"are no edge indices."}],"type":"paragraph"}]}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The complement of a graph, as a lazy view. Made by "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"complement"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/GraphOperations\/documentation\/GraphOperations"]]},"topicSections":[{"generated":true,"title":"Structures","identifiers":["doc:\/\/GraphOperations\/documentation\/GraphOperations\/ComplementView\/Cell","doc:\/\/GraphOperations\/documentation\/GraphOperations\/ComplementView\/CellRow","doc:\/\/GraphOperations\/documentation\/GraphOperations\/ComplementView\/DirectedEdges","doc:\/\/GraphOperations\/documentation\/GraphOperations\/ComplementView\/IndexRow","doc:\/\/GraphOperations\/documentation\/GraphOperations\/ComplementView\/UndirectedEdges"],"anchor":"Structures"},{"generated":true,"title":"Instance Properties","identifiers":["doc:\/\/GraphOperations\/documentation\/GraphOperations\/ComplementView\/base"],"anchor":"Instance-Properties"},{"generated